WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.389
Section 10-126 lists what can cost Theo his license.

00:00:04.389 --> 00:00:08.777
Making an intentional material misstatement on a license application.

00:00:08.777 --> 00:00:13.166
Obtaining or trying to obtain a license through fraud.

00:00:13.166 --> 00:00:17.067
Violating the Insurance Article or a Commissioner’s order.

00:00:17.067 --> 00:00:23.406
Misappropriating, converting or unlawfully withholding money belonging to an insurer, insured or applicant.

00:00:23.406 --> 00:00:26.819
Intentionally misrepresenting the terms of a policy.

00:00:26.819 --> 00:00:28.770
Forging an insurance document.

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:34.133
Being convicted of a felony or a crime of moral turpitude.

00:00:34.133 --> 00:00:37.547
Demonstrating a lack of trustworthiness or competence.

00:00:37.547 --> 00:00:41.448
Failing to obey a lawful order or subpoena.

00:00:41.448 --> 00:00:44.861
Having a license disciplined in another state.

00:00:44.861 --> 00:00:47.299
Failing to report as required.

00:00:47.299 --> 00:00:51.200
Any of these supports action against the license.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:55.674
After notice and an opportunity for a hearing, the Commissioner may refuse

00:00:55.674 --> 00:01:00.149
to issue or renew a license, place the licensee on probation with

00:01:00.149 --> 00:01:03.877
conditions, suspend the license for a period, or revoke it.

00:01:03.877 --> 00:01:08.351
Each has a different effect: a suspended producer may return when the

00:01:08.351 --> 00:01:12.453
period ends; a revoked producer must reapply and may be refused.

00:01:12.453 --> 00:01:15.809
Instead of, or in addition to, suspension or revocation,

00:01:15.809 --> 00:01:19.164
the Commissioner can impose a fine for each violation.

00:01:19.164 --> 00:01:20.656
The minimum is $100.

00:01:20.656 --> 00:01:22.147
The maximum is $5,000.

00:01:22.147 --> 00:01:26.994
The Commissioner may also order restitution for someone who suffered a financial loss.

00:01:26.994 --> 00:01:31.469
The Commissioner may also issue a cease-and-desist order requiring a person to

00:01:31.469 --> 00:01:35.570
stop a specific unlawful practice, with further penalties for ignoring it.

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:37.272
Due process shapes the procedure.

00:01:37.272 --> 00:01:41.358
Before most license sanctions, the Commissioner gives written notice of the proposed

00:01:41.358 --> 00:01:45.103
action and the grounds, and the licensee may demand a hearing.

00:01:45.103 --> 00:01:49.189
Under Section 2-210, a written hearing demand states the grounds for the

00:01:49.189 --> 00:01:53.274
request; the Commissioner must grant and hold the hearing within 30 consecutive

00:01:53.274 --> 00:01:57.360
days, unless the parties agree to postpone it, or refuse it, and

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.424
a failure to act is treated as a refusal.

00:02:00.424 --> 00:02:04.509
At the hearing the producer may appear, present evidence and be represented.

00:02:04.509 --> 00:02:06.893
The Commissioner then issues a written order.

00:02:06.893 --> 00:02:09.616
Where the public faces immediate danger, the law

00:02:09.616 --> 00:02:12.340
permits summary action followed by a prompt hearing.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:15.620
A producer who loses before the Commissioner is not finished.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:19.228
Under Section 2-215 a petition for judicial review in the circuit

00:02:19.228 --> 00:02:22.835
court is generally due within 30 days after the order is

00:02:22.835 --> 00:02:25.787
served, or after a refusal to hold a hearing.

00:02:25.787 --> 00:02:28.739
The court reviews the record for legal error and

00:02:28.739 --> 00:02:31.363
substantial evidence; it does not retry the case.

00:02:31.363 --> 00:02:34.971
Filing the petition does not automatically stay the Commissioner’s order, so

00:02:34.971 --> 00:02:38.579
a revoked producer stays revoked unless the court grants a stay.

00:02:38.579 --> 00:02:41.858
Theo’s practical lesson: respond to every notice, keep records that

00:02:41.858 --> 00:02:45.138
show what actually happened, and never ignore a lawful order

00:02:45.138 --> 00:02:48.090
or request for records while a matter is pending.

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:53.110
Insurance fraud is knowingly presenting a false or misleading statement, claim

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:57.674
or document to obtain a benefit, premium, commission or coverage.

00:02:57.674 --> 00:03:02.238
It includes padding a disability claim, backdating an application, pocketing

00:03:02.238 --> 00:03:06.346
a premium, or helping a client misstate health history.

00:03:06.346 --> 00:03:12.279
Maryland prosecutes insurance fraud criminally, with penalties that rise with the amount involved

00:03:12.279 --> 00:03:18.212
and can include prison, and the Commissioner can add administrative penalties and restitution.

00:03:18.212 --> 00:03:22.776
The Insurance Fraud Division, created under Section 2-401 within the

00:03:22.776 --> 00:03:26.884
Administration, investigates suspected fraud and refers cases for prosecution.

00:03:26.884 --> 00:03:30.535
Producers are frequent targets of fraud and frequent

00:03:30.535 --> 00:03:33.730
sources of the evidence that stops it.

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:39.336
Section 27-802 requires a person with good-faith cause to believe insurance fraud has

00:03:39.336 --> 00:03:44.943
occurred to report it in writing; an independent producer reports to the Fraud

00:03:44.943 --> 00:03:50.549
Division, and producers working for an insurer report through the insurer’s anti-fraud process.

00:03:50.549 --> 00:03:55.724
Good-faith reports are protected from civil liability; knowingly false accusations are not.

00:03:55.724 --> 00:04:01.762
When Theo suspects a client inflated a claim, he does not quietly withdraw and

00:04:01.762 --> 00:04:07.800
hope; he preserves the facts, follows the reporting process and cooperates with the Division.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:11.250
Concealing suspected fraud to protect a commission is

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:14.700
itself a path to discipline under Section 10-126.
